In this lesson, we'll look at how to pick the right function family when you're modelling a set of numbers. The big idea is simple, just look at how consecutive values change, and that change tells you what kind of model fits. First up, the constant difference test. Subtract each term from the one after it. If those differences are all the same number, you've got a linear model, the classic straight line. Next, the constant second difference test. If the first differences aren't constant, take the difference of the differences. If those second differences are constant, then your data follows a quadratic shape, a parabola. Finally, the constant ratio test. Instead of subtracting, divide each term by the one before it. If that ratio stays the same every step, you're dealing with an exponential model, where values grow by multiplying. So remember, subtract for linear, subtract again for quadratic, and divide for exponential. Now let's see it in action.